 what is this thing you know  what's different about it what is it  with the Sun or two you don't have that  challenge of getting it because a lot of  people are already excited about it you  have other challenges how much do you  reinvent it because if it's too familiar  people are like ah feels like a 1.5 if  it's too different they're like wow you  deviated from the pattern that I love  and I can tell you from experience  that's a really tricky balance I happen  to think dishonor to was a fantastic  sequel it did a great job of as Smith  said being different  without being too different it took the  ideas set forth by its predecessor  introduced new mechanics into the fold  and just went deeper with the series  gameplay tenants but other sequels  haven't fared as well take the recent  Middle Earth shadow of war for instance  it's an extremely fun Lord of the Rings  game at its best it uses its clever  Nemesis system to let you find your own  story among the politics of more doors  orc armies but the rest of the time I  actually think shadow of war tried to do  too much it bogs it's more brilliant  moments down with baggage there are  weapons - fine armor together  collectables to hoard and a marketplace  plagued with microtransactions it didn't  completely forget what made the original  great to begin with but it didn't  exactly complement it either on the  other end of the spectrum we have the  recent Assassin's Creed origins as with  shadow of war origins is a good game in  its own right however I think Ubisoft's  newest entry in the long-running  franchise should have done more than it  does it reworks the combat mechanics a  little and attempts to open its world up  to  or exploration than its predecessors but  the resulting experience still feels a  bit stale as was the case with  Assassin's Creed syndicate and unity  before it origins often tasks you with  side quests that don't add to the game  they're just sort of they're these shaky  steps make it all the more apparent how  much of a masterclass Wolfenstein 2 is  in sequel creation it amplifies the  qualities of the first game and polishes  most of its flaws it also adds its own  systems that make it a stellar journey  on its own without sacrificing that  Wolfenstein feel many people are  expecting I'll trust you blaskowitz  there's nothing saying you couldn't be  wearing a mask made it look like William  J blessed wish to hide your alien space  lizard facial traits so I mean the past  week grace gave you she didn't give me  one right off with a mask  show me your lizard face [ __ ]  super special lower your weapon front  break your armor but you're like the new  Colossus begins almost immediately after  the new orders ending our hero BJ  blaskowitz is clinging to life after his  battle with general death said he slowly  recovers and once again he's called upon  to kill some Nazis I won't go too much  into spoiler territory from this point  forward but if you want to play the new  Colossus from a completely fresh  perspective pause the video go play it  and come back in a couple days I promise  it's well worth your time  [Music]  anyway  [Music]  wolfenstein to story is fantastic not  just because of its own momentum but how  it develops already established  characters and plot points in a  meaningful way it goes back in time to  show us BJ's childhood it explores how  his Jewish mother and abusive father  clashed it shows us how BJ got to this  point and suggests how he might move  forward it develops existing  relationships it creates new ones and it  destroys others and despite Wolfenstein  z-- focus on shooting and stabbing this  entry isn't afraid to explore the  aftermath of those things the trauma and  exhaustion that come with constant  fighting and violence at one point in  the new Colossus BJ breaks down and  literally can't do it anymore this  patriotic macho man admits that he  cannot go on how he manages to do so is  another story entirely and it  encapsulates just how batshit crazy the  new Colossus is willing to become  speaking of this super also marks a  tonal shift as well although I love the  new order it's somber tone often felt  heavily at odds with its murdering Nazis  gameplay this time around however  developer machinegames has embraced the  bombast of its narrative don't get me  wrong there are plenty of dark moments  throughout the sequel but the new  Colossus finds a better balance between  swagger and desperation it's characters  are at once touching and hilarious the  new Colossus is an uprising fuelled by  tender moments and machine-gun riffs  alike and here it's all of a piece this  is a video game however and a good story  isn't always enough on its own for the  sequel machine games needed to iterate  on gameplay mechanics pacing and design  as well and here too the team succeeded  [Music]  the new order had a variety of pacing  problems too often relying on sandbox  environments to let you choose between  stealth or a louder approach the new  Colossus however hurls a plethora of  scenarios at you over the course of its  campaign it still does a great job of  allowing for different plans of attack  but it does so in more creative ways you  have access to three contraptions or  these mechanical superpowers that let  you navigate levels through brute force  sneak attacks or higher vantage points  the combat is about more than shooting  and cover it's also about using the  environment to your advantage it's about  asking yourself questions and answering  them quickly is this Factory better  suited for a close-quarters combat do I  have enough ammo to take down that  panzer hood can I cross this gap with my  current health or should I stock up  before making my run the new Colossus  also offers a handful of optional side  missions at your HQ and in the field  they lead to additional upgrades for BJ  but they also help the pacing they're  great bookends to the spectacle of each  main mission they're excuses to return  to areas you've already visited with new  objectives and aesthetics in mind you  can find new routes through the  battlefield  you can keep your enemies at a distance  this time around or you can avoid them  completely opting we'll need to kill  when you absolutely need to although the  new order tried to let you play at your  own pace the new Colossus has actually  refined that tactic and it's a better  overall experience because of it I don't  envy developers having to rely on  sequels as Smith said in regards to  Dishonored creating a new IP is its own  challenge but expanding upon it while  also retaining existing fans is another  problem entirely Studios run the risk of  doing too much they run the risk of not  doing enough the best sequels however  find ways to strike that balance the new  Colossus is a shining example it  developed its characters while also  asking us to reconsider them it  maintains the gritty feel of the new  order while also amplifying it this time  around it hones its pacing structure and  mechanics creating a superb experience  in the long run it's an instant classic  and any sequel moving forward should  take no  [Music]  you

